A||N||9||Zorba||Hydraulics - 1st & 2nd Design...Huh?||||11||
Z||000000||Zorba||08-20-2000||08:01 PM||||I am looking to replace both my slave and my master clutch cylinders on my 86SE. Now, the fiero store has a 1st and 2nd design master cyl which is basically defined by where the <BR>reservoir is located. Middle of front of pipe. Well, the one that was in my car was the 1st design and I am wondering if it was placed there incorrectly by the 1st owner thus causing my not so smooth engagement problem. Is there anyway to tell which one actually came with the 86 SE? Because if it has been replaced incorrectly (easy to do)<BR>then it could explain my bad clutch engagement outside of other possibilities.<BR>There is also a DIFFERENCE in price. the second design is CHEAPER. Carparts.com has them even cheaper than the fiero store ones.<BR>I also have no idea what brand transaxle I have. It's a 5 speed but I don't know if it is ISUZU or Getrag Or Muncie? How do you know? The fork on my tranny is unlike the Getrags. It's looks like to panes of metal bracing the spot the piston pushes against.<BR>Thanks for any clarification for me and others here with similar challenges.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Zorba||08-20-2000||08:04 PM||||I found this description at the fiero store... but it doesn't tell me if performance or specs are affected. Is aluminum better than cast iron? Does it matter where the reservoir is located?<P>---&gt;The Fiero Store carries new Wagner clutch master and slave cylinders. These units are made to original GM specifications. NOTE: 1st design master cylinders are aluminum and the reservoir is positioned toward the front of the car. 2nd design cylinders are cast iron/steel and the reservoir is positioned in the middle of the master cylinder. CHECK OUT THE TOOLS PAGE TO MAKE THE JOB EASIER! &lt;--------||||reg||5
Z||000002||mrfiero||08-20-2000||08:07 PM||||While I'm not positive, I believe that an '86 SE with a 5-sp. would have the second design.  Also, I don't believe it is possible to mix the two up....that is, I don't think a first design one will work on a Fiero using the second design.  I think the hydraulic lines are different.<P>Check the RPO codes on the inner fender well.  Look for MG2......this is the getrag code.  Also, look at the shift cables and how they are routed.....the Isuzu is completely different from the Getrag.  <P>One other possibility is that it has a FWD Getrag tranny.....that would explain its different appearance. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||08-20-2000||08:10 PM||||One more thing......buy genuine GM hydraulics!  Even though Wagner claims to build them to GM specs, they are more prone to failure.  I had one last less than one year (went through 3 slaves in 6 months).  <P>It will save lots of future headaches and is well worth the extra $$$.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||08-20-2000||08:18 PM||||Is there a GM parts web site that would sell the same thing?  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Zorba||08-20-2000||08:44 PM|||| I found my RPO CODE...<P>I guess I have an MT2 - ISUZU 5 speed tranny<BR>with a an FX4 - 3.35 final gear ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||08-20-2000||10:12 PM||||That makes sense......I just saw your other post saying it was a 4-cyl. SE.  I just assumed it was a V-6.  <P>I don't think there is a parts warehouse online, but I'm not sure.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||pherder||08-20-2000||10:17 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  is the url for a GM parts place that I have ordered from in the past.  <P>VERY good prices on the parts ... VERY high prices on their shipping.<P>BTW, the site hasn't loaded on my machine since yesterday ...<P>Paul||||reg||1
